so on [0,1] we have y in [0,2/3] (1.5 periods) \n#   plus y in [2/3,1] (= [0,1/3]).\n# \n# Let me carefully analyze G on [0,1].\n# G has period 2/3. On [0,2/3]: 3 maxima (at 1/9, 2/9, 1/3) and 4 minima.\n# Wait, let me recount from the computation:\n# Maxima at 0.111, 0.167, 0.222, 0.333, 0.444, 0.5, 0.556 (7 maxima on [0,2/3])\n# Minima at 0.077, 0.140, 0.193, 0.257, 0.410, 0.473, 0.527, 0.590 (8 minima on [0,2/3])\n# \n# Hmm, that's a lot. Let me reconsider.\n# Actually, the maxima at 0.167, 0.333, 0.5 are the \"seam\" values where G=1 (the endpoints of the fundamental domain copies).\n# And 0.111, 0.222, 0.444 are the interior maxima.\n# \n# Let me reconsider the fundamental domain of G.\n# G(y)=4g(f(cos(3pi y))).\n# cos(3pi y) has period 2/3.\n# cos(3pi y)=cos(3pi(2/3-y)) by the reflection cos(theta)=cos(-theta).\n# So G has reflection symmetry y->2/3-y.\n# Fundamental domain [0,1/3].\n# On [0,1/3]: cos(3pi y) goes from 1 to -1.\n#   f(cos(3pi y)) = | |cos(3pi y)| - 0.5 |\n#   At y=0: cos=1, f=0.5\n#   At y=1/6: cos=0, f=0.5\n#   At y=1/3: cos=-1, f=0.5\n#   So f=0.5 at the endpoints and middle.\n#   In between, f varies.\n#   f is minimized when |cos(3pi y)|=0.5, i.e., cos(3pi y)=±0.5.\n#     cos(3pi y)=0.5: 3pi y = pi/3, so y=1/9. f=|0.5-0.5|=0.\n#     cos(3pi y)=-0.5: 3pi y = 2pi/3, so y=2/9. f=|0.5-0.5|=0.\n#   So f=0 at y=1/9 and 2/9, and f=0.5 at y=0,1/6,1/3.\n# g(f)=|f-0.25|.\n#   At f=0: g=0.25, G=1.\n#   At f=0.5: g=0.25, G=1.\n#   g is minimized when f=0.25.\n#     f=0.25: |cos(3pi y)|-0.5=±0.25, so |cos(3pi y)|=0.75 or 0.25.\n#       cos(3pi y)=0.75: 3pi y=arccos(0.75)=0.7227, y=0.0767. g=0, G=0.\n#       cos(3pi y)=-0.75: 3pi y=pi-arccos(0.75)=2.4189, y=0.2500. g=0, G=0.\n#       cos(3pi y)=0.25: 3pi y=arccos(0.25)=1.3181, y=0.1399. g=0, G=0.\n#       cos(3pi y)=-0.25: 3pi y=pi-arccos(0.25)=1.8234, y=0.1935. g=0, G=0.\n#   So G=0 at y=0.0767, 0.1399, 0.1935, 0.2500.\n# \n# So on [0,1/3]: G goes 1 (at 0) -> 0 (at 0.0767) -> 1 (at 1/9=0.111) -> 0 (at 0.1399) \n#   -> 1 (at 1/6=0.167) -> 0 (at 0.1935) -> 1 (at 2/9=0.222) -> 0 (at 0.25) \n#   -> 1 (at 1/3=0.333).\n# That's 3 maxima (at 1/9, 1/6, 2/9) and 4 minima (at 0.0767, 0.1399, 0.1935, 0.25).\n# \n# So on [0,1/3], G has 3 maxima and 4 minima.\n# On [0,1] (1.5 periods), G has 3+3=6 interior maxima and 4+4=8 interior minima, \n#   plus the seam maxima at 1/3, 2/3, 1.\n# \n# This is getting complicated. Let me just carefully count the intersections numerically.\n# \n# The key insight: G(F(x)) oscillates, and we need to count how many times it crosses x.\n# Let me use a very fine grid and count sign changes, being careful about the structure.\n\n# Let me analyze the oscillation of G(F(x)) near a specific point.\n# Take x=0.003. F(0.003)=?\nx = 0.003\nprint(f\"F({x})={F(x)}\")\n# G(F(0.003))=?\nprint(f\"G(F({x}))={G(F(x))}\")\n# The crossing at x=0.003 means G(F(0.003))=0.003.\n# But F(0.003) is some value, and G of that value is 0.003.\n# Since G ranges in [0,1], G(F(x))=0.003 is possible.\n\n# Let me think about this more carefully.\n# The crossings come in pairs because of the grid resolution.\n# Let me use a much finer grid and see if the count stabilizes.\n\n# Actually, let me reconsider the whole approach.\n# The problem is that G(F(x)) is highly oscillatory, and counting its zeros is hard.\n# Let me think about the structure of the intersection.\n\n# C1: y=F(x). C2: x=G(y).\n# Intersection: (x, F(x)) on C1 and (G(y), y) on C2, so x=G(y) and y=F(x).\n# \n# Let me think about the \"inverse\" of C1. C1 is y=F(x). The inverse relation is x=F^{-1}(y),\n# but F is not invertible. \n# \n# Let me think about it as: the number of (x,y) with y=F(x) and x=G(y).\n# = number of (x,y) with F(x)=y and G(y)=x.\n# \n# Let me count this by considering the curves as sets and counting intersections.\n# \n# Actually, let me just carefully count using the structure.\n# Let me discretize C1 and C2 finely and count intersections.\n\n# Let me use a different approach: count intersections of the two curves in [0,1]^2.\n# C1: (x, F(x)) for x in [0,1].\n# C2: (G(y), y) for y in [0,1].\n# \n# Let me parametrize C2 by y and C1 by x, and count intersections.\n# For each x, C1 is at (x, F(x)). C2 passes through (x, y) where G(y)=x.\n# So the intersection at x is where F(x)=y and G(y)=x, i.e., G(F(x))=x.\n# \n# Let me count the number of solutions of G(F(x))=x for x in [0,1].\n# \n# The issue is the oscillation. Let me think about the total number of times G(F(x)) crosses x.\n# \n# Let me use a fine grid and count sign changes, then verify the count is stable.\nxs = np.linspace(0, 1, 4000001)\nFvals = F(xs)\nHvals = np.array([G(Fv) - xv for xv, Fv in zip(xs, Fvals)])\nsign_changes = 0\nfor i in range(1, len(xs)):\n    if Hvals[i] * Hvals[i-1] < 0:\n        sign_changes += 1\nprint(f\"Sign changes of H over [0,1] (fine grid): {sign_changes}\")\n\n# Let me also count with a coarser grid to see the trend.\nfor n in [100001, 200001, 400001, 800001]:\n    xs2 = np.linspace(0, 1, n)\n    Fvals2 = F(xs2)\n    Hvals2 = np.array([G(Fv) - xv for xv, Fv in zip(xs2, Fvals2)])\n    sc = sum(1 for i in range(1, len(xs2)) if Hvals2[i]*Hvals2[i-1]<0)\n    print(f\"n={n}: sign changes = {sc}\")"}) - MainThread
